  "narrative": "The delivered validation materially strengthens the PTEN-loss and MSI-high claims and resolves the SMARCA4 lineage-interaction follow-up. For H49, PTEN loss in samples intact for TP53, KEAP1, and SMARCA4 has discovery signed estimate +0.252 [0.169, 0.335] and independent validation signed estimate +0.281 [0.159, 0.403]. Both intervals exceed the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed stronger-dependency direction. This is the cleanest PTEN robustness evidence: the moderate PTEN association is not explained by co-occurrence with the large TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, or SMARCA4-loss states. I accept H49 and close it as a completed conditional robustness check, while retaining the broader PTEN claim H44 with a caveat because the overall PTEN validation lower bound is slightly below threshold. For H82, MSI-high in the TP53-/KEAP1-/SMARCA4-intact stratum has discovery +0.745 [0.666, 0.825] and independent validation +0.779 [0.675, 0.884]. These are large, precise, and far above threshold. I accept H82 and close this specific independence check; the MSI-high association is not an artifact of co-occurring TP53, KEAP1, or SMARCA4 loss. The scheduled validation for SMARCA4 loss by adenocarcinoma interaction is also important. H55 tested a negative interaction in the original direction and remains unsupported. H65, the reversed reverse-direction interpretation, was positive in discovery (+0.174 [0.035, 0.312]) but lower than threshold, and independent validation is much smaller and imprecise (+0.084 [-0.113, 0.281]). Therefore neither direction supports a meaningful adenocarcinoma-specific modification of the SMARCA4-loss association. The direct adenocarcinoma estimate H41 remains accepted (+0.760 [0.686, 0.833]), so the scientific conclusion is broad robustness of the SMARCA4 association rather than lineage-specific heterogeneity. Overall supported dataset-level findings are: TP53 loss is associated with higher/less-negative composite scores, i.e. weaker dependency, with signed main effect +0.829 [0.767, 0.891]; KEAP1 loss likewise associates with weaker dependency, signed +0.810 [0.745, 0.875] and validated +0.788 [0.698, 0.879]; SMARCA4 loss associates with stronger dependency, signed +0.804 [0.742, 0.867] and validated +0.800 [0.705, 0.895]; MSI-high status associates with much stronger dependency, signed +0.775 [0.664, 0.885] and validated +0.842 [0.673, 1.010]; and PTEN loss has a moderate stronger-dependency association, signed main effect +0.239 [0.145, 0.332] and validated +0.279 [0.138, 0.419], best supported by the independently validated triple-intact stratum H49. Negative or unsupported findings remain important: no meaningful associations were found for STK11 loss, KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, MTAP loss, ARID1A loss, RB1 loss, SMAD4 loss, BRCA2 loss, PIK3CA mutation, APC mutation, NF1 loss, media serum percentage, or the three research markers. EGFR amplification produced only a weak opposite-direction signal and remains unsupported as a stronger-dependency predictor. Most interaction tests involving the accepted predictors, including TP53-by-KEAP1, lineage, screen-quality, and co-occurrence contexts, do not support meaningful multiplicative heterogeneity. Unresolved uncertainty remains mainly around PTEN subgroup-specific estimates, the small MSI-by-KEAP1 interaction, untested drivers ERBB2 amplification and BRAF V600E, batch and Cas9 technical interactions, and arbitrary research signatures. The most informative next analyses are direct batch-stratum robustness checks for PTEN and MSI-high, existing MSI robustness in Avana and high screen-quality screens, PTEN adenocarcinoma and high-Cas9 strata, and broad driver exploration of ERBB2 and BRAF. All claims remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal gene-specific knockout effects or externally generalizable biological claims."
